WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.670
Five teachers, five classes, and

2
00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:06.170
a head teacher who says: the most

3
00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:07.670
senior choose first.

4
00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:10.540
It sounds fair, and it’s quick.

5
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:12.960
But what happens to the

6
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.420
teacher who chooses last?

7
00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:18.070
Today: the best plan, and

8
00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:19.030
who it’s best for.

9
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.640
Each teacher has a score for each class,

10
00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:26.320
from zero to nine: how well they suit it.

11
00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.540
Amina is good at almost

12
00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:30.860
everything, and loves maths.

13
00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.930
Karim is really good only at maths.

14
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.360
Each class needs one teacher, and

15
00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.300
each teacher takes one class.

16
00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.840
Amina chooses first and takes

17
00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.200
maths, her favourite.

18
00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.320
The others choose in turn, each

19
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:49.400
taking their best class still free.

20
00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:51.540
Karim is left with science,

21
00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:53.080
a two out of nine.

22
00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.040
The total is twenty-nine.

23
00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:59.220
Now give maths to Karim, science to

24
00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.100
Leila, English to Yusuf, art to

25
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.140
Zainab and history to Amina.

26
00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:06.300
The total is thirty-nine,

27
00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:07.700
the best there is.

28
00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:11.090
And the lowest score is six, not two.

29
00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.510
So how do you judge a plan?

30
00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:17.920
Look at the total: how good it is overall.

31
00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:20.630
Look at the worst-off: the

32
00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.390
lowest score anyone gets.

33
00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:24.430
And look at the gap between

34
00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:25.650
the best and the worst.

35
00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:29.650
A plan can win on one and lose on another.

36
00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:31.550
Quick check!

37
00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.100
Under first come, first

38
00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.420
served, who comes off worst?

39
00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.860
Karim, with a class he can hardly

40
00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:42.260
teach, and his pupils lose too.

41
00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:45.150
But the better plan asks Amina to give up

42
00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:48.070
her favourite: she drops from nine to six.

43
00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:52.030
That’s a real cost to her, so ask it

44
00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:55.250
openly, and fairly, don’t just order it.

45
00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:58.140
Several rules sound fair.

46
00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:00.940
First come, first served is quick,

47
00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:03.300
but an early choice can take

48
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.680
someone’s only good option.

49
00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:07.790
The best total is good for the school,

50
00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:10.150
but someone may give up a favourite.

51
00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.140
Looking after the worst-off makes the

52
00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:15.500
lowest score as high as it can be.

53
00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.030
Whatever rule you use, agree it

54
00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:20.730
with the people affected before

55
00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:22.030
anyone knows who wins.

56
00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:24.880
Say the rule out loud and

57
00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:25.780
show the numbers.

58
00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:29.100
Let people correct a score that’s wrong.

59
00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:32.070
People accept a plan they can understand.

60
00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:36.420
In the app, press Let them choose in turn,

61
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:39.160
and see first come, first served.

62
00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.140
Then press Run and watch the

63
00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:44.560
Hungarian method find the best total.

64
00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:46.630
Change Karim’s maths score,

65
00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:48.290
and run both again.

66
00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:50.070
Your turn.

67
00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:53.220
For class duties, the plan with the

68
00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:55.500
best total gives one pupil the job

69
00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.780
nobody wants, every single week.

70
00:02:58.420 --> 00:02:59.140
Is that fair?

71
00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:02.860
Not necessarily: a great total

72
00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:04.880
can still leave one person with

73
00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:06.700
the worst job every week.

74
00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.480
Remember: judge a plan by its

75
00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.280
total, its worst-off and its gap.

76
00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:15.040
First come, first served can

77
00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.180
waste good matches.

78
00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:18.760
And agree the rule with the

79
00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:19.540
people it affects.

80
00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:22.340
Try it in class tomorrow:

81
00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:24.360
plan class duties fairly.

82
00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.970
Pupils score each job from one to five.

83
00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:31.430
Compare first come, first served with

84
00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:33.810
a plan that lifts the lowest score,

85
00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.490
and rotate the hardest job.

86
00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.140
Then ask the class which plan

87
00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:40.760
they’d choose, and why.
